<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="Campagnolo Cambio Corsa: the shifting system that required the rider to release the rear wheel while moving."/><title>Campagnolo Cambio Corsa · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="components" danishPath="/komponenter/campagnolo-cambio-corsa/"/><main><article class="cambio-page">
<header class="cambio-hero"><p class="section-label">Campagnolo · Italy · 1946</p><h1>Cambio Corsa</h1><p>A gear change demanding courage and balance — the rider released the rear wheel while moving.</p></header><figure class="cambio-main-image"><img src={`${images}/two-levers.webp`} alt="Two Cambio Corsa levers on the Stella Veneta"/><figcaption><em>Due leve</em> — two levers that demanded practice.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="cambio-opening"><p class="section-label">A mechanical intermediate step</p><p>After the Second World War the racing bicycle changed rapidly. Cambio Corsa allowed shifting without dismounting, though its operation now appears remarkably risky.</p></section>
<section class="cambio-story"><aside><strong>due leve</strong><span>Italian for<br/>“two levers”</span></aside><div><p>Campagnolo introduced it in 1946. It moved both chain and rear wheel.</p><p>With no sprung derailleur, the axle moved in toothed horizontal dropouts to compensate for changing chain length.</p><blockquote>All while the bicycle was moving.</blockquote></div></section>
<section class="cambio-mechanism"><div><p class="section-label">The shift</p><h2>Right hand backwards.<br/>Rear wheel loose.</h2></div><div class="cambio-steps">{steps.map(([n,t,x])=><div><span>{n}</span><h3>{t}</h3><p>{x}</p></div>)}</div></section>
<section class="cambio-risk"><div><p class="section-label">Courage and balance</p><h2>A poor shift could cost more than speed</h2></div><div><p>The rider reached backwards, released the wheel and back-pedalled, often on rough roads or in a peloton. Incorrect locking could leave the wheel displaced or crooked.</p><p>“Suicide gear” is a later nickname, not clearly a contemporary one. Professionals treated it as a competition tool offering a real advantage when mastered.</p><p>Chain force pulled the wheel forwards and the brake guided it back. The sequence had to be exact.</p></div></section>
<figure class="cambio-race"><img src={`${images}/race.webp`} alt="Rider reaching towards the levers" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The rider reached down while rolling.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="cambio-era"><p class="section-label">Three or four sprockets</p><blockquote>Modest now.<br/>A major step <em>then.</em></blockquote><div><p>Previously riders stopped and moved the chain by hand or reversed the wheel. Cambio Corsa allowed forward motion during the change.</p><p>Gino Bartali and Fausto Coppi are associated with this generation of Italian shifting systems.</p></div></section>
<section class="cambio-coppi"><div><p class="section-label">Resisting the myth</p><h2>Coppi, Bartali and the Tour</h2></div><div><p>They did not contest a Cambio Corsa duel in the 1948 Tour’s Pyrenees: Coppi did not start. Bartali led and won after his Alpine attacks.</p><p>In 1949 they travelled as joint leaders. Coppi won Giro and Tour, but sources indicate he also used French Simplex components; the Tour victory cannot simply be credited to Cambio Corsa.</p></div></section>
<section class="cambio-gallery"><figure><img src={`${images}/stella-veneta.webp`} alt="Stella Veneta with Cambio Corsa" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The collection’s Stella Veneta.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${images}/drawing.webp`} alt="Technical drawing" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Lever, guide, hub and dropouts formed one system.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="cambio-evolution"><div><p class="section-label">What followed</p><h2>Paris–Roubaix and Gran Sport</h2></div><div><p>A single-lever model appeared in 1949, later known as Paris–Roubaix after Coppi’s 1950 victory. The axle still had to be released.</p><p>Gran Sport provided the lasting solution: a fixed wheel and sprung derailleur that moved and tensioned the chain automatically.</p></div></section>
<section class="cambio-closing"><blockquote>Not merely a “suicide gear” —<br/>but a bold step towards <em>the modern racing bicycle.</em></blockquote></section><nav class="era-bottom-nav"><a href={`${base}/en/components/`}>← All components</a><a href={`${base}/en/components/shimano-600-ex-arabesque/`}>Shimano 600 EX Arabesque →</a></nav></article></main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
